 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Count Burc Akyol as one designer who is all-in on the stirrup revival: he’s been doing them for several seasons now—long enough in fashion time for it to be called a house signature.</p><p>“People love it for its dynamic elasticity,” the designer offered during a backstage preview. Turns out, such elasticity was the baseline for a spring show based almost entirely on jersey, and mostly on iterations of the catsuit, high-arced in the stirrups with a “caryatid” torsade at the navel.</p><p>The designer called that flourish his “couture” touch, a way of taking a universally loved material, stretching it to its limits and making it feel special; he also injected structure with shoulder pads, adding to the overall glamazon vibe. Akyol has serious tailoring chops, but for spring even blazers and trench coats had the fit of dresses—with, as the designer quipped, the suppleness of yoga outfits. Even the “denim” was jersey. So was that dramatic draped white bomber. And a couple of bustiers that will get a lot of mileage.</p><p>Akyol can find inspiration everywhere he looks. Some of it, he explained, came from summers spent on Pantelleria—a terracotta number mimicked the tan lines one might get from lolling too long in the sun. Called “Milk and Wine,” the collection meant to bookend a universal early life experience and the spiritual, ritualistic associations of libation, but it also picked up on threads having to do with family, roots, love and loss, and memories of trips to places like Cairo and Tokyo. One example: Silver was rarer than gold in ancient Egypt, hence both were used on a trench coat with a 3D-printed belt. Another: a seat cover in a taxi inspired a shift dress made of wooden beads because, after all, clothes go out into the world and engage with people in ways that are intimate, mysterious and unpredictable. In this particular case, they can very literally leave a mark.</p><p>The collection notes also tagged Man Ray, <em>Blade Runner</em>, Art Deco, voyeurism and Akyol’s grandmother, a cabaret singer in the 1960s. And on it went, like an AI-generated transcript. Then came: <em>s’habiller d’un rien</em>—to dress in next to nothing. That one pretty much said it all: these clothes were made for women who want to strut their stuff.</p></div>",
